<node id="11132165" title="Re^12: Perl Contempt in My Workplace" created="2021-05-06 13:31:44" updated="2021-05-06 13:31:44" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;p&gt;Just my two cents:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;Can anyone provide a definitive reference on the meaning of version 1.0 in the Perl world, especially for CPAN modules?&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I've always thought it was pretty arbitrary, that is, TIMTOWTDI...&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;advice on how a CPAN module should advertise that it is stable, well-tested and not experimental.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That's a good question. Personally I would make a note in the POD and/or Changelog if a version is stable - though I tend to only release decently-tested stuff anyway. If I was giving advice to someone else, what you quoted from SemVer is probably good advice. And I wrote a bit about judging the quality of a CPAN module [id://1168649|here]. (&lt;i&gt;Update:&lt;/i&gt; [hippo] also makes a good point [id://11132187|here].)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;Oh, and other cool Perl versioning references you may know of that are not mentioned here are also welcome.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My bookmarks on versioning modules currently are: [1182640] and [11114665].&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
